course: dictionary
lesson: 2980
title: Who is a duchess?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A duchess is a woman with a very high rank. She is married to a duke, or she holds the rank of duke herself. Clues in the words around the word help you see this.
explain: In the news you may read about «the Duchess of Cambridge» or «the Duchess of Sussex». The words «of» and a place tell you her title, so she is a very grand person.
explain: In «Alice in Wonderland», the Duchess speaks in a grand way, and the White Rabbit is worried she will be «savage» if he keeps her waiting. Read the clues, then check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence.
fact: A duchess is a woman who is married to a duke, or who holds the rank of duke herself.
fact: «Duchess» is sometimes used in a friendly or teasing way to speak to a woman who seems grand.
fact: «The Duke and Duchess» and «the Duchess of …» are common phrases in the news and in history.
fact: «Your Grace» is a polite way to speak to a duchess.
fact: In «Alice in Wonderland», the Duchess says, «Everything's got a moral, if only you can find it.» A moral is the lesson a story teaches.
exercise: What is a duchess? | A girl who has just learned to read | *A woman married to a duke, or a woman who is a duke herself | A servant who cooks in a castle | A man who rules a country
exercise: In the news, «the Duchess of Sussex» is a woman with what? | *A very high rank | A pet cat | A fast car | A job selling fish
exercise: Your friend grins and says, «Thanks, Duchess!» after you tidy your room like a queen. What does it probably mean? | A warning to stop talking | A real title, so you are now a duke | A rule about tidy rooms | *A friendly, teasing way to talk to someone who seems grand
test: In «the Duke and Duchess», which word means a woman married to a duke? | Queen | *Duchess | Duke | Knight
test: In «Everything's got a moral, if only you can find it», what is a moral? | *A lesson a story teaches | A type of hat | A cook's knife | A loud noise
exam: Which phrase from the lesson is a polite way to speak to a duchess? | *Your Grace | Oi, sunshine | Hey you | Miss Mouse
